 class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>A god is just a monster you kneel to. </b>- ArcaneArts, Quoting &quot;Fall of Gods&quot; <div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/personality.htm\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://www.nodiatis.com/pub/6.jpg\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a> </div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7920-master-aquatosic","name":"Master Aquatosic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7920_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2369"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-10-21T13:34:20-07:00","id":1261980,"title":"","content":"In which Calamity hears about a new Demon, and Lori meets an interesting new person. Insincere Marguerite meets an old flame and Jacob goes hiking.<br /> <br /> <i>Session XIX</i><br /> <br /> <b>Lorelei\u2019s Diary</b><br /> <br /> Grrrr. I had a very pleasant evening last night, and up until a few minutes ago I <u><u>was</u></u> anticipating another such evening tonight, but nooooo\u2026 Calamity had to sign the motley up for <u><u>another</u></u> demon-hunting field trip to L.A. And yes, I could totally blow this excursion off in favor of an evening of carnal delights \u2013 I think Becky actually <u><u>wants</u></u> me to do that in fact \u2013 but I know I\u2019d feel terrible if anything happened to any of my friends and I wasn\u2019t there to provide the requisite healing.<br /> <br /> So I ended my last entry as we were prepping for the Friday night show. Showtime rolled around and <u><u>boy</u></u> was this one a doozy. The show\u2026 well, the show was <u><u>good</u></u> of course, with me debuting a new number, Marguerite returning to her \u201cGirls Like Girls\u201d number, and Calamity revisiting an old favorite with the \u201cStuck In the Middle With You\u201d chair dance thingummy, but that\u2019s not really what I meant. The show was special mostly because of who was there.<br /> <br /> First among these guests was Yvette, who was finally back from her vacation and hoping to reconnect with Marguerite following their extended time apart (it had been about seven months since they had been together from Yvette\u2019s perspective). I was first alerted to her presence when I caught the two of them backstage about a half an hour before curtain, frenching (naturally) like a couple of high-school sweethearts under the bleachers at the homecoming game.<br /> <br /> The next \u2013 and most obvious \u2013 newcomer to our little cabaret earned a number of nicknames over the course of the evening. \u201cUnibomber,\u201d \u201cNazgul,\u201d \u201cDarth Vader\u2026\u201d the list goes on and on. He sat alone in a shadowy corner, wearing the darkness around him like a shroud. Nobody sat at any of the adjacent tables, even going so far as to move chairs to already crowded locations just to avoid his general vicinity. Though it was difficult to get a good look at him, he was massive, clothed all in black, and wearing a dark hood under which no hint of his face could be seen, even from a short distance away.<br /> <br /> His shadowy mantle identified him as Night Court, so just prior to curtain Calamity and I climbed up into the catwalks to ask Marguerite about him. She told us that his name was \u201cMr. Gone,\u201d and that he acts as sort of an enforcer or regulator for the Warlock., helping to keep L.A.\u2019s Night Courtiers in line and making sure that the Court\u2019s interests are protected. He\u2019s known as a <u><u>very</u></u> dangerous individual, but Marguerite was relatively certain that he wasn\u2019t at our club to make trouble\u2026 had that been the case, he likely wouldn\u2019t have made his presence so obvious.<br /> <br /> So I resolved to speak with him during the act break after I debuted my big number. Little did I know that a few other new faces had trickled into the crowd while I was backstage. Sitting together at a table between the stage and the bar were an older man in a somewhat threadbare suit, a buff young guy with a soulpatch in a tight t-shirt, and a cute young blonde with a killer body. I\u2019d last seen them in downtown L.A., stripping, firing a gun in the air, and being pulled from a burning car, respectively.<br /> <br /> Well, the fact that all three of them were at our club \u2013 <u><u>together</u></u> \u2013 didn\u2019t smell like a coincidence to me. Add to that the fact that their eyes seemed to follow me every time I showed myself, and you have a recipe for disaster. It was enough to make me consider changing my number\u2026 but we already had the audio and lighting cued up, so it was really too late for a last-minute substitution by that point.<br /> <br /> Calamity\u2019s acts came up before mine. Daring as always, she actually picked the young guy to join her on-stage for her chair dance act. When she used her cobblethought magic to make it look like she\u2019d severed one of his ears, the crowd went wild, but the older guy seemed genuinely upset before he realized that it had all been a trick.<br /> <br /> My own act eschewed the microphone for once and focused on dance. I strutted out on stage wearing lacy lingerie including a <u><u>really</u></u> cute garter and stocking combo down south, and swayed and sashayed around the stage to the strains of the J. Geils Band\u2019s \u201cCenterfold.\u201d Most of the crowd ate it up; I got a very good response from the audience in general. The three mortal newcomers seemed a bit nonplussed, though if they really do retain some memory of what happened last Tuesday, that might just be due to my choice of song, and the fact that the contracts of Vainglory had once again made my wings visible to mortals.<br /> <br /> I decided to ignore them for the time being, hoping they would lose interest and wander off on their own. Instead I threw a kimono on over my lingerie and motioned for Calamity and Becky to join me at Mr. Gone\u2019s table, and strode across the room as confidently as I could. He greeted us with a wave, and spoke in a deep, powerful voice.<br /> <br /> \u201cGood evening, Your Majesty,\u201d he said simply. \u201cYou have quite a club here. You are most talented. Please, have a seat.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cThank you,\u201d I said, Calamity and I seating ourselves while Becky stood protectively behind us. \u201cYour words are most kind. Mr. Gone, I presume?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cIndeed,\u201d he said. \u201cI come representing the Night Court of L.A., in the hopes that we can deescalate the current\u2026 \u2018misunderstanding\u2019 between our respective peoples.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cI hope so as well,\u201d I said sincerely. \u201cNow, introductions. I am Mistress Lorelei de la Rocha, of course. This is Calamity, a member of my motley, and Becky, my claviger.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cCharmed,\u201d he said, inclining his\u2026 uh, hood. \u201cI confess, I came here for several reasons. First, I was curious about our neighbors\u2019 new monarch. Second, I felt that my presence here, at a known sanctuary, would be less likely to be misinterpreted as a threat than if I had simply walked into the reigning monarch\u2019s place of business. And finally\u2026 I had hoped to enjoy a pleasant evening at your cabaret. Thus far I have not been disappointed.\u201d<br /> <br /> So we chewed over the situation for a while. Gone lamented the foolishness of his fellow Night Courtiers in attacking T.J., whom he described as \u201cbasically a plus-sized wookalar.\u201d I had no idea what he was talking about, but tried my best to play it off as a witticism with a laugh and a smile, and that seemed to satisfy him.<br /> <br /> Both of us were reasonably well-informed as to the facts of recent events, though there were surprises on both sides. Gone told me, for instance, that the changeling who seemed to have been the ringleader of the attack on T.J. was unfortunately in a deep coma, and unlikely to emerge in the near future. I offered my court\u2019s healing services as a gesture of goodwill, and Calamity offered to step into the man\u2019s dreams to see if she could glean any more information from him, but Mr. Gone declined both offers for the time being.<br /> <br /> \u201cYou are most generous,\u201d he said after we had made these offers, \u201cand I will pass your words along to the Warlock. For now we would prefer to handle these events ourselves, but we will contact you if that should change.\u201d<br /> <br /> We had a bit of information that came as a surprise to Gone as well\u2026 namely that it was Olivia Gold who first alerted me to the situation. Gone found that tidbit <u><u>very</u></u> interesting. \u201cThat woman is a viper,\u201d he opined, \u201cand Leonidas gave her the ambassador position mostly to remove her from his own house. It was one of the wisest things he has ever done.\u201d<br /> <br /> Gone is of the opinion that Olivia, knowingly or not, may have served as a cat\u2019s-paw for the Hollywood Courts in this matter. It\u2019s hard to argue against his logic; Hollywood definitely has the most to gain from sowing discord between L.A. and the Lady By the Sea. With the threat of the Courts of Fallen Stars and the Grand Maw looming over us, none of us can afford to fight a war on multiple fronts. Gone offered his assistance in our investigation of Olivia, but I decided to show the same discretion he had offered me.<br /> <br /> \u201cI thank you for your generous offer,\u201d I said, \u201cand I will pass your words along to King Lowjack. But my opinion is that he will probably want to handle this matter in-house. Of course I\u2019ll contact you if that changes.\u201d We exchanged numbers, and parted cordially.<br /> <br /> Of course I headed backstage to a private room and called John pretty much immediately to let him know about our strange and puissant visitor. After ascertaining that I did not need his assistance with Gone, he backed my decision to handle the investigation privately. He did ask a favor of me\u2026 he wanted my motley to \u201clet slip\u201d to both Olivia Gold and Willow Black that Mr. Gone is interested in this situation. Hopefully the threat of the Night Court\u2019s private boogeyman will spook them into cooperating with Lowjack\u2019s people more readily than they normally might.<br /> <br /> Lowjack also passed along an interesting rumor about Mr. Gone. Apparently there\u2019s a sort of folk legend about him. People believe that if you talk about him too much, or say his name too often in a single day, he\u2019ll appear. The odd thing is that the Wyrd seems to have picked up on this story and run with it\u2026 it\u2019s actually become <u>true</u> over the years. So\u2026 you know what, I\u2019m not going to talk about this anymore right now. Y\u2019know, just in case.<br /> <br /> I made the rounds after that, studiously avoiding the table with the trio from the rush hour incident. Yvette asked for advice on her upcoming meeting with Boss Lowjack (she was planning to introduce herself to John the following day as a courtesy, since she\u2019s spending so much time in our Freehold). I told her to just be honest with Lowjack, as he\u2019d probably see through any lies she might tell in seconds flat anyway. I also advised her that Marguerite\u2019s presence at the meeting wouldn\u2019t be strictly necessary, but probably wouldn\u2019t hurt either.<br /> <br /> I was chatting pleasantly with Jolie and Desiree when I noticed Calamity moving across the floor with purpose. A moment later I saw why. The gun-toting anarchist from L.A. was making a beeline for Mr. Gone\u2019s table. I don\u2019t know why he thought that might be a good idea, but I can only assume that Calamity took it upon herself to stop him for his own good.